   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 524242,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 89770,
    "poster_id": 11062,
    "post_rendered": "<b>Problem 3.</b> Two points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\"  width=\"16\" height=\"12\" > are chosen inside a non-equilateral triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > such that <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/a/a5af701b9c1de5e09560a74208458c02c003b10e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAM=\\angle CAN$\"  width=\"142\" height=\"13\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/c/22ceacafec5be225a02a3fb53b2a35c28c355214.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABM=\\angle CBN$\"  width=\"143\" height=\"13\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/f/d0f05315482043323ebf2e01711050f2485cd453.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AM\\cdot AN\\cdot BC=BM\\cdot BN\\cdot CA=CM\\cdot CN\\cdot AB=k\\]\"  width=\"440\" height=\"13\" > for some real <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" >.</span> Prove that:<br>\n<br>\n<b>a)</b> We have <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/e/e3e0766cd8a31297dfdf3c5f9e78479b0d3015b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3k=AB\\cdot BC\\cdot CA$\"  width=\"155\" height=\"13\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<b>b)</b> The midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/c/bbcfabd65745f2b759dea1840f9a0753b2f992e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MN$\"  width=\"36\" height=\"12\" > is the medicenter of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\"  width=\"58\" height=\"13\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<i>Remark.</i> The <b>medicenter</b> of a triangle is the intersection point of the three medians:<br>\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/6/d3645fa769fd8028b5fb6939c519333027340042.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" > is midpoint of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/5/97531e1861626687838b2e7ff029fc9d18c69871.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" > of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/0/010dd7e68eb27f1ce5b462841ea650bffe2169f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" > of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" >,</span> then <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/b/13b4da5e4e021fff2e29061910a61cf5ff5beb79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AA_{1}\\cap BB_{1}\\cap CC_{1}= G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"184\" height=\"15\" >,</span> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > is called medicenter of triangle <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<i> Nikolai Nikolov</i>",
    "post_canonical": "[b]Problem 3.[/b] Two points $M$ and $N$ are chosen inside a non-equilateral triangle $ABC$ such that $\\angle BAM=\\angle CAN$, $\\angle ABM=\\angle CBN$ and \\[AM\\cdot AN\\cdot BC=BM\\cdot BN\\cdot CA=CM\\cdot CN\\cdot AB=k\\] for some real $k$. Prove that:\r\n\r\n[b]a)[/b] We have $3k=AB\\cdot BC\\cdot CA$.\r\n\r\n[b]b)[/b] The midpoint of $MN$ is the medicenter of $\\triangle ABC$.\r\n\r\n[i]Remark.[/i] The [b]medicenter[/b] of a triangle is the intersection point of the three medians:\r\nIf $A_{1}$ is midpoint of $BC$, $B_{1}$ of $AC$ and $C_{1}$ of $AB$, then $AA_{1}\\cap BB_{1}\\cap CC_{1}= G$, and $G$ is called medicenter of triangle $ABC$.\r\n\r\n[i] Nikolai Nikolov[/i]",
    "username": "imortal",
    "post_number": 1,
