    "topic_id": 89771,
    "poster_id": 11062,
    "post_rendered": "<b>Problem 4.</b> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > be the circumcircle of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\"  width=\"58\" height=\"13\" >,</span> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\"  width=\"15\" height=\"12\" > the point on the arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/4/c04203b1ea012d729d4788a7309144c33514f476.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overarc{AB},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"23\" > which do not pass through <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" >.</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/2/822b800ca9993bd5686e4fe3a128c4659d3e979d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/f/e4f8fcfe896fcde9d2732076f4f3c3a94cf78f14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" > are the centers of incircles of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/e/b8eabc87afe4c11a47e4aa27628d5517af4866b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ADC$\"  width=\"59\" height=\"13\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/7/1a7a0327109ede54bf3abf9eb325bb58a503afc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BDC$\"  width=\"60\" height=\"13\" >,</span> respectively. Proove that the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/685cd6cbe43567009ef10f74cd8ad5e28c86b524.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle I_AI_BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"68\" height=\"16\" > touches <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > iff <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/c/6ecd7f774aa8c836e91371236aab9b8f9fd7221c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[ \\frac{AD}{BD}=\\frac{AC+CD}{BC+CD}.  \\]\"  width=\"145\" height=\"39\" ><br>\n<br>\n<i> Stoyan Atanasov</i>",
    "post_canonical": "[b]Problem 4.[/b] Let $k$ be the circumcircle of $\\triangle ABC$, and $D$ the point on the arc $\\overarc{AB},$ which do not pass through $C$. $I_A$ and $I_B$ are the centers of incircles of $\\triangle ADC$ and $\\triangle BDC$, respectively. Proove that the circumcircle of $\\triangle I_AI_BC$ touches $k$ iff \\[ \\frac{AD}{BD}=\\frac{AC+CD}{BC+CD}.  \\]\r\n\r\n[i] Stoyan Atanasov[/i]",
    "username": "imortal",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_11062.jpg"
