    "post_id": 524231,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 89763,
    "poster_id": 11062,
    "post_rendered": "<b>Problem 3.</b> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/1/811f76ab716762dc2652ba611378a1816b671893.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\geq 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" > is given natural number, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > is the set of the first <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > primes. For any nonempty subset <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\"  width=\"15\" height=\"12\" > of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/b/7eb3955b3897cf58a1709a54fc2496e0773f132f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(X)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" > denote the product of its elements. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\"  width=\"16\" height=\"12\" > be a set of the kind <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><span class=\"aopscode-error aopscode-latex-error\">$\\ds\\frac{P(A)}{P(B)}$</span>,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/4/244b5c3aaf405d42e064d7ea5910f3926ca63a4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A\\subset M, B\\subset M, A\\cap B=\\emptyset$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"211\" height=\"18\" > such that the product of any 7 elements of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\"  width=\"16\" height=\"12\" > is integer. What is the maximal number of elements of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\"  width=\"16\" height=\"12\" >?</span><br>\n<br>\n<i>Alexandar Ivanov</i>",
    "post_canonical": "[b]Problem 3.[/b] Let $n\\geq 3$ is given natural number, and $M$ is the set of the first $n$ primes. For any nonempty subset $X$ of $M$ with $P(X)$ denote the product of its elements. Let $N$ be a set of the kind $\\ds\\frac{P(A)}{P(B)}$, $A\\subset M, B\\subset M, A\\cap B=\\emptyset$ such that the product of any 7 elements of $N$ is integer. What is the maximal number of elements of $N$?\r\n\r\n[i]Alexandar Ivanov[/i]",
